"end":482,"topicId":"drawing"}],"cards":[{"time":415.64,"title":"Parametricism","id":"parametricism"},{"time":428.24,"title":"Xerox","id":"xerox"},{"time":445.16,"title":"Grand Central Station","id":"grand-central-station"}]}],"cards":{"Alison and Peter Smithson":{"slug":"alison-and-peter-smithson","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England\">English</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architects</a> Alison Smithson (22 June 1928 – 16 August 1993) and Peter Smithson (18 September 1923 – 3 March 2003) together formed an architectural partnership, and are often associated with the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Brutalism\">New Brutalism</a> (especially in architectural and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_theory\">urban theory</a>). Peter was born in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stockton-on-Tees\">Stockton-on-Tees</a> in north-east England, and Alison was born in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheffield\">Sheffield</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Yorkshire\">South Yorkshire</a>. They met while studying architecture at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durham_University\">Durham University</a> and married in 1949. Together, they joined the architecture department of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_County_Council\">London County Council</a> before establishing their own partnership in 1950.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1903.76,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"}]},"Cedric Price Office":{"slug":"cedric-price-office","body":"","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":444.04,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1751.88,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":43.4,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":1.64,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":314.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":442.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":184.12,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":247.32,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1065.2,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1063.84,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":272.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":181.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":466.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":26.88,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":716.28,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":743.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1639.24,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":2.64,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":568.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":672,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":761.16,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"}]},"FUN PALACE":{"slug":"fun-palace","body":"Fun Palace is Cedric Price’s most celebrated work. Whether characterised as a giant toy or as a building-sized transformable machine, the project’s interest resides in its radical reliance on structure and technology, its exemplification of notions of time-based and anticipatory architecture. With Fun Palace, Price addressed social and political issues that go far beyond the typical bounds of architecture. Fun Palace was conceived and commissioned in 1961 by renowned theatre director and producer Joan Littlewood. Price developed plans for the project through 1964, both for the main project and for a smaller, more mobile “pilot” project. Neither were realised. Attempts to get planning permission for a wide variety of sites within and around London continued through 1970, amidst opposition from church, citizen groups and confounded city councils.","subtitle":"Laboratory of Fun and a University of the Streets","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/fun-palace.jpg","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/283-cedric-price-fun-palace","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.518722,-0.0007261,13z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":139.88,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":464.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":647.24,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":11,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":381,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":671.76,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1034.84,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1547.08,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":414.28,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1021.72,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1036.76,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1132.72,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":873.92,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1684.84,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":744.8,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":796.08,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":883.28,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":676.6,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1117.36,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1903.76,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":651.64,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":83.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":138.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":637.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":681.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1943.6,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1104.36,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"}]},"Grand Central Station":{"slug":"grand-central-station","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Grand Central Terminal (GCT) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commuter_rail\">commuter</a> (and former intercity) railroad <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminal_station\">terminal</a> at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/42nd_Street_(Manhattan)\">42nd Street</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Avenue_(Manhattan)\">Park Avenue</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midtown_Manhattan\">Midtown Manhattan</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City\">New York City</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States\">United States</a>. Built by and named for the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Central_Railroad\">New York Central Railroad</a> in the heyday of American long-distance passenger rail travel, it is the largest such facility in the world by number of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Railway_platform\">platforms</a> with 44 serving 67 tracks along them. They are on two levels, both below ground, with 41 tracks on the upper level and 26 on the lower, though the total number of tracks along platforms and in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rail_yards\">rail yards</a> exceeds 100. The terminal covers an area of 48 acres (19 ha).</p>","subtitle":"Grand Central Terminal","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/grand-central-station.jpg","external":"http://www.grandcentralterminal.com","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/grand+central+terminal/@40.752726,-73.977229,15z","also":[{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":445.16,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"}]},"Nick Barley":{"slug":"nick-barley","body":"Nick Barley worked in London as a publisher and editor of arts books and magazines throughout the 1990s. The magazines under his stewardship included the design magazine Blueprint; and Tate magazine. During this period he also edited more than 40 books including Breathing Cities and Re:CP – Cedric Price. In 2003 Nick moved to Scotland to become editor of the cultural magazine The List, and he was 2005 PPA Editor of the Year. From 2006-2009 he was director of The Lighthouse – Scotland’s Centre for Architecture, Design and the City, where he commissioned a major Scottish Pavilion by Gareth Hoskins for the Venice Architecture Biennale.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;","subtitle":"Director, Edinburgh International Book Festival","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/nick-barley.jpg","external":"http://www.conradfestival.pl/en/3/632/780/nick-barley","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/United+Kingdom","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":572.28,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"}]},"Parametricism":{"slug":"parametricism","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Though parametricism has its roots in the digital animation techniques of the mid-1990s, it has only fully emerged in recent years with the development of advanced parametric design systems. Parametricism has become the dominant, single style for avant-garde practice today. It is particularly suited to large-scale urbanism as exemplified by a series of competition-winning master-plans by Zaha Hadid Architects.</p>","subtitle":"Patrick Schumacher and friends","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/parametricism.jpg","external":"http://www.patrikschumacher.com","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Montreal,+QC,+Canada/@45.5086699,-73.5539925,7z","also":[{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":415.64,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"}]},"Xerox":{"slug":"xerox","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">A photocopier (also known as a copier or copy machine) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine\">machine</a> that makes <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paper\">paper</a> copies of documents and other visual images quickly and cheaply. Most current photocopiers use a technology called <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xerography\">xerography</a>, a dry process that uses electrostatic charges on a light sensitive photoreceptor to first attract and then transfer toner particles (a powder) onto paper in the form of an image. Heat, pressure or a combination of both is then used to fuse the toner onto the paper. (Copiers can also use other technologies such as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inkjet_printer\">ink jet</a>, but xerography is standard for office copying). Xerographic office photocopying was introduced by <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xerox\">Xerox</a> in 1959, and it gradually replaced copies made by <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Verifax&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\">Verifax</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photostat\">Photostat</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_paper\">carbon paper</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimeograph_machine\">mimeograph machines</a>, and other <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duplicating_machines\">duplicating machines</a>. The prevalence of its use is one of the factors that prevented the development of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paperless_office\">paperless office</a> heralded early in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_revolution\">digital revolution</a>.</p>","subtitle":"Copy machine","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/xerox.jpg","external":"www.xerox.com","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/search/xerox+norwalk/@41.118618,-73.434124,13z","also":[{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":428.24,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"}]}}}